        "title": "Forest Ecology: Succession, Gap Dynamics, Nutrient Cycling",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A forest is not a stable assemblage — it is a system perpetually recovering from disturbance at multiple spatial and temporal scales. The central organizing frameworks are: (1) succession theory (directional change after disturbance), (2) gap dynamics (the forest as a mosaic of patches at different successional stages), and (3) nutrient cycling (the biogeochemical loops that sustain productivity)."
    }
